(4) Prompt-vs-reality contradictions documented (precedent: phase-42a/0011)

  Prompt 0014 problem statement: "8 routes: pressure (Pa, SI from raw psi or kPa via SettingTirePressureUnit) per corner, plus optional status/sensor-id columns."
  Reality: 8 routes split as 4 pressure + 4 last_seen_at TIMESTAMPTZ (NOT status/sensor-id). routing.yaml lines 917-922 explicitly assign the epoch->TIMESTAMPTZ conversion to this writer. status/warning columns are populated downstream from signal_log per lines 889-895.

  Prompt 0014 Decision #5: "1 positive (text status)" — IMPOSSIBLE because no *_status field is routed under dest: tire_pressure_snapshot. Substituted with "1 positive (epoch -> time.Time)" which exercises the actual writer-owned conversion path.

  Prompt 0014 Decision #2: "Composition: snapshotWriter with table = ..., columnFor map" — preserved for the 4 pressure (DOUBLE PRECISION) routes; cannot apply to the 4 last_seen_at (TIMESTAMPTZ) routes because snapshotWriter.bindSnapshotValue rejects time.Time. Hybrid wrapper keeps SQL shape, identifier sanitisation, error-prefix, and PII-clean unknown-vehicle behaviour byte-for-byte identical to snapshot_base for log/classifier parity. Rubber-duck design review (sync) confirmed the hybrid is the minimal correct interpretation under the locked allowed-files scope (snapshot_base.go is NOT modifiable).

  Prompt 0014 problem statement: "Per ADR-004 #9, the SI value arriving at this writer is ALREADY in Pascals (the unit conversion happened in normalize.toSI). The writer just persists; it does not re-convert." — CORRECT for the 4 pressure routes (UnitKindPressure -> normalize.toSI converts to Pa). The 4 last_seen_at routes are UnitKindNone so normalize.toSI is a pass-through; the writer owns the float64-epoch -> time.Time conversion as routing.yaml documents.

(5) protomodel signal metadata for the 8 routed fields (verbatim from internal/tesla/protomodel/signal_metadata_gen.go)

  TpmsPressure{Fl,Fr,Rl,Rr}              ValueKindFloat, UnitKindPressure   -> normalize.toSI emits float64 in Pa
  TpmsLastSeenPressureTime{Fl,Fr,Rl,Rr}  ValueKindFloat, UnitKindNone       -> normalize.toSI pass-through; writer owns epoch->TIMESTAMPTZ

=== IMPLEMENTATION ===

Files added (allowed-files scope):
  - internal/tesla/router/writers/tire_pressure_writer.go
  - internal/tesla/router/writers/tire_pressure_writer_test.go

Locked decisions applied (mirroring prompt 0012/0013, with documented adaptations for the timestamp branch):

  #1 Constructor: NewTirePressureWriter(pool *pgxpool.Pool) router.Writer; nil pool panics with
     "pool must be non-nil"; snapshot constructor errors panic with the wrapped error.
  #2 Composition: newSnapshotWriter(pool, "tire_pressure_snapshots", tirePressureColumnFor) is
     embedded as the .snap field; pressure writes delegate to it. The 4 last_seen_at routes
     take a writeTimestamp branch that mirrors snapshot_base.go SQL/identifier/error contracts
     byte-for-byte except for the time.Time bind. See AUDIT_EVIDENCE (4) for rationale.
  #3 Map source-of-truth: static tirePressureColumnByField (8 entries) NOT runtime YAML re-read.
     A second package-level set, tirePressureTimestampColumns (4 entries), drives the
     writeTimestamp dispatch — keyed on column name so the dispatch is decoupled from the
     producer-side proto field identifier.
  #4 Reflective coverage: TestTirePressureWriter_ColumnMapMatchesRoutingYAML walks
     router.LoadMap() and asserts (len==8) + (no missing) + (no extra) + (column matches) for
     DestTirePressure. Secondary drift gate
     TestTirePressureWriter_TimestampColumnsCoverTimestamptzColumns asserts every entry in
     tirePressureTimestampColumns has a corresponding routed field.
  #5 Tests (substituted per AUDIT_EVIDENCE #4):
     - TestTirePressureWriter_TypeMatrix: float64 pressure (TpmsPressureFl=222000.0 Pa) +
       float64 epoch (TpmsLastSeenPressureTimeFl=1746541200.0 -> time.Unix(1746541200,0).UTC()).
     - TestTirePressureWriter_FractionalEpochPreserved: 1746541200.25 -> nanos preserved
       (uses 0.25 because it has an exact binary float64 representation).
     - TestTirePressureWriter_UnknownFieldReturnsError: VehicleSpeed -> "no column mapping",
       zero db calls; field/table appear in error for log correlation.
     - TestTirePressureWriter_TimestampUnsupportedValueType: string/int64/bool/nil at the
       timestamp branch -> "unsupported value type" with the snapshotWriter[...]:< field>
       prefix, zero db calls.
     - TestTirePressureWriter_InvalidEpochValuesRejected: NaN, +Inf, -Inf, negative -> typed
       error per branch; zero db calls; same prefix.
     - TestTirePressureWriter_ZeroEpochAccepted: zero epoch is intentionally NOT rejected
       (routing.yaml does not constrain it as a sentinel; rubber-duck Q5).
     - TestTirePressureWriter_TimestampVehicleNotRegistered: RowsAffected==0 path for the
       timestamp branch -> "vehicle not registered", VIN excluded (PII), table+field present.
     - TestTirePressureWriter_TimestampDBExecErrorWrapped: errors.Is unwrap chain preserved.
     - TestNewTirePressureWriter_NilPoolPanics: ctor fail-fast contract.

Reasoning notes:

  - Snapshot tables hard_warnings/soft_warnings columns are NOT routed by this writer because
    the per-corner hard/soft warning bool atomics route to signal_log (routing.yaml lines
    889-895): the snapshot writer reads the per-corner change stream from signal_log to
    populate the per-corner *_status columns and the rolled-up *_warnings tokens. That
    roll-up logic lives in a downstream materialiser, NOT this prompt's allowed files.
    Schema columns intentionally not routed by this writer: front_left_status,
    front_right_status, rear_left_status, rear_right_status, hard_warnings, soft_warnings.

  - coerceEpochToTime uses math.Modf + math.Round + nsec==1e9 carry to preserve sub-second
    precision and avoid systematic truncation when the float64 epoch is close to a second
    boundary (rubber-duck issue #2). Rejects NaN, +/-Inf, negative, and overflow > MaxInt64.
    Accepts zero (not a sentinel under routing.yaml).

  - VIN-resolution contract inherited from snapshot_base (prompt 0010): codec.Atomic.VehicleID
    is the VIN string, not numeric vehicles.id. Both the embedded snap (pressure path) and
    the writeTimestamp branch use the same INSERT...SELECT FROM vehicles WHERE v.vin = $1
    sub-query for the lookup. PII-clean RowsAffected==0 message preserved verbatim.

  - Per-corner suffix convention Fl/Fr/Rl/Rr preserved in column names; Semi tractor's
    dual-rear-axle per-tyre fields (Re1L0/Re1L1/Re2L0/... per routing.yaml lines 760-770)
    are NOT routed here — they land in signal_log because tire_pressure_snapshots only
    models the four passenger-car corners.

  - Rubber-duck design review (sync; tire-pressure-writer-design): all 6 blocking +
    non-blocking recommendations adopted: identifier sanitisation in writeTimestamp (#1),
    math.Modf rounding (#2), byte-for-byte SQL parity with snapshot_base (#3), test
    substitution documented in AUDIT_EVIDENCE (#4), reject only negatives/non-finite (#5),
    add unsupported-type test for timestamp path (#6), add RowsAffected==0 test for
    timestamp path (#7), fractional-seconds test (#8). Concurrency confirmed safe (#10):
    only immutable fields after construction.

